Use controlled infinite sums in a stipulated energy ladder (@physics-statistical-countable: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-equilibrium-weights-from-a-law-of-relaxation-and-sampling.lingua#r_00EE492F15A9D8710F97287B36") { r_0020301215067F6BEA7F416700
A finite spectrum always has a finite normalizer at finite β. An unbounded spectrum requires a convergence argument. Consider two labeled independent modes with stipulated levels E_(i,j)=ε(i+j), i,j=0,1,…, ε>0, no level degeneracy beyond these pairs and no interaction term. These may motivate a later oscillator model, but the spectrum and its independent-mode approximation are premises here; no quantum spectral derivation is claimed. A common constant energy offset changes the reference U, not the probabilities or heat capacity at fixed offset.

For T>0, r=exp[−ε/(k_B T)] lies in (0,1). The mathematical work in [Normalize countable weights without assigning probability from counts alone](../../mathematics/core/Normalize-countable-weights-without-assigning-probability-from-counts-alone.lingua#r_009B007FED35B6ED29D669B6FA) gives Z=1/(1−r)², mean total excitation 2r/(1−r), and variance 2r/(1−r)². Restore energy units to get U=2εr/(1−r) and Var(E)=2ε²r/(1−r)². The justified series operations and parameter bounds remain in [Justify exchanging a limit with an integral or derivative](../../mathematics/core/Justify-exchanging-a-limit-with-an-integral-or-derivative.lingua#r_001E30FA67E83674F48C0427F2). A finite numerical table alone cannot justify the infinite model or its derivatives.

With x=ε/(k_B T), C=2k_B x²r/(1−r)². As T grows, x→0 and (1−exp(−x))/x→1 from the derivative of exp at zero, giving C→2k_B and U/(k_B T)→2. As T→0 from above, polynomial factors times exp(−x) vanish, so U and C tend to zero for this model. These limits do not determine real material heat capacity when further modes, interactions, phase changes or temperature-dependent levels matter. At β=0, r=1 and the infinite normalizer diverges, unlike a finite two-state system. The high-temperature limit is approached through valid positive β; it is not a normalized distribution at β=0.

The same distribution gives S/k_B=−2ln(1−r)−[2r/(1−r)]ln r. This follows from −Σp ln p using the finite first moment; it is not obtained by counting a fixed-energy shell and ignoring energy fluctuations. For m independent identical modes the means and variances add and Z multiplies, but a constraint on their total or an interaction may destroy the product form.

To approximate the two-mode distribution, reuse [Separate omitted mass from the error of a normalized average](../../mathematics/core/Separate-omitted-mass-from-the-error-of-a-normalized-average.lingua#r_0011B5DB162366534860F2D9BE) exactly. Conditioning on i+j≤N removes a tail and renormalizes the remaining probabilities. At r=1/2,N=3, missing probability is 3/16, missing first moment is 1, and the conditional mean is 16/13 versus the full mean 2. Thus the energy-mean error is 10ε/13. The first-moment certificate is ε times the mathematical certificate. At N=13 the missing first moment is 121/16384, below 1/100, so it certifies energy-mean error below ε/100. A probability-tail tolerance alone cannot certify an unbounded energy observable.

**Work:** recover the thermodynamic identities with the shared convergence proofs, compare finite two-state and infinite-ladder boundaries, and change the two energy spacings to unequal positive values. Specify a physically testable premise that would be needed before treating either ladder as a material model. Use the existing `series_study.py` from [Investigate normalization truncation and the limits of finite evidence](../../mathematics/core/Investigate-normalization-truncation-and-the-limits-of-finite-evidence.lingua#r_004A475C6869E7393BE0AECA72); do not replace its tail certificate with a plot that looks converged.

**Continue:** [Separate equilibrium weights from a law of relaxation and sampling](Separate-equilibrium-weights-from-a-law-of-relaxation-and-sampling.lingua#r_00EE492F15A9D8710F97287B36).

